    </header>
    <section class="page-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">CHRONICLES OF THE FORGE</p>
        <h1>Every ember leaves a mark.</h1>
        <p>New threats, balance changes, buffs, nerfs, and fixes. Follow how the watch evolves.</p>
    </section>

    <article id="patch-004" aria-labelledby="patch-004-title">
        <header class="patch-heading">
            <div class="patch-meta"><span class="badge">PATCH 004</span><span class="badge latest">LATEST</span><a href="/patch-notes#patch-004" target="_self" aria-label="Link to patch 004">Permalink ↗</a></div>
            <h2 id="patch-004-title">Power Under Pressure</h2>
            <p>A balance pass to keep growing characters powerful without making later stages effortless. Enemy progression, class identities, survival resources, and Endless pacing have been reworked.</p>
            <div class="tags"><span>Balance</span><span>Class identity</span><span>Ranger</span><span>Survival</span><span>Loot</span><span>Endless</span></div>
        </header>
        <section class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">COMBAT CLARITY &amp; FAIR DAMAGE</p><h3>Stronger decisions, clearer limits</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Ranger special and burst casts now have independent per-target hit budgets: the first arrow hit retains full damage, the next two retain 25% each, and further hits from that cast deal no damage. Piercing follow-through shares this budget. Other enemies retain their own full first hit, preserving crowd clearing without point-blank boss deletion. Regular attacks and their single ricochet are unchanged.</li>
                <li>Bosses advance phases at two-thirds and one-third health. Phase one uses slams; phase two alternates slams and aimed five-projectile fans; phase three adds an eight-projectile radial pattern. Phases never reverse when a boss is healed.</li>
                <li>Every boss special has a 1.1-second matching warning and locked aim. After it resolves, bosses stop moving and attacking for 1.5 seconds, or two seconds after a radial pattern. Phase transitions add a 1.5-second opening. Already-fired projectiles remain dangerous during recovery.</li>
                <li>Forge and post-forge buttons preview effective current → next damage, cooldowns, armor, health, healing, speed, critical stats, and reach as appropriate. Current buffs and automatic-slot cooldown penalties are included. Capped stats and purchases with no current stat gain are labeled rather than hidden; some capped forge ranks may still be needed to unlock training.</li>
                <li>At 25% health or lower, an available flask briefly highlights. Drinking remains manual. Empty or cooling-down flasks do not flash, and reduced-motion mode uses a static highlight.</li>
                <li>A fixed-height combat-pressure row shows stage, remaining enemies, elites, active elite traits, level-scaling status, and boss phase/recovery. Full text is available on hover without adding a click-blocking canvas overlay.</li>
                <li>Failed checkpoint saves now expose a prominent <strong>Retry checkpoint save</strong> button inside the overlay. Retry uses the existing save protection against duplicate requests, preserves the character on failure, and advances Endless only after a successful save.</li>
                <li>Save format 14 retains boss phase, warning pattern, recovery, and per-cast arrow hit budgets. Earlier formats remain loadable; saved in-flight arrows are grouped conservatively by skill when no cast identity exists.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">PATCH BOARDS &amp; BUILD EXPERIMENTS</p><h3>A fresh competition, room to rebuild</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Rankings now default to <strong>Patch 004 — fresh runs</strong>. A patch filter exposes the archive without mixing it with new records. Difficulty, starting mode, and class remain separate within each patch.</li>
                <li>Older records migrate to the archive with their scores, dates, and build snapshots intact. Older clients submit to that archive by default. Saves created before patch tracking remain inherited runs—even after entering Endless—so accumulated old power cannot enter the fresh-run boards. Start a new campaign or veteran Endless run to qualify for Patch 004.</li>
                <li>Added a reset button to each skill tree: <strong>100 gold + 50 per spent point</strong>, so a fully trained tree costs 500 gold. Confirm while paused or at a checkpoint to refund that tree's points. Other trees, equipment, forge ranks, cards, and loadout remain unchanged; spent gold is not refunded.</li>
                <li>Respecs preserve cooldowns and remove refunded active protection, regeneration, mobility effects, or arrows. Resetting a ward imposes its normal protection gap. Empty trees cannot be reset. Checkpoint changes autosave; elsewhere use Save or reach the next checkpoint.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">ELITE TRAITS &amp; DEATH REPORTS</p><h3>Read the threat, learn from the hit</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>From wave 11, eligible non-boss elites have a <strong>35% chance</strong> to gain one trait. No trait stacking: labels, symbols, and colored rings identify the threat, and the skills panel explains each trait.</li>
                <li><strong>Armored:</strong> takes 20% less damage, without reflecting attacks. <strong>Swift:</strong> walks 18% faster, without speeding up charges or shortening warning animations. <strong>Mending:</strong> regenerates 1.5% maximum health per second while alive; concentrate damage to defeat it.</li>
                <li>Summoned ordinary enemies do not inherit elite traits. Saving and loading retains the same traits without rerolling them.</li>
                <li>Death now shows the killing blow plus up to 12 hits from the last eight seconds of combat. Reports identify melee strikes, projectiles, charges, slams, explosions, mortars, and ground hazards—including elite traits where applicable.</li>
                <li>Reported damage is actual health lost after armor and protection, capped at remaining health on the lethal hit. Invulnerable hits are omitted; healing is not subtracted. Pauses do not age the log. Death history is session-only and clears on load or a new run; projectile attribution survives saving.</li>
                <li>Save format 13 stores patch eligibility, elite traits, and projectile sources. Formats 1–12 remain supported; old enemies gain no retroactive traits and old projectiles use an explicit older-save source label.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">PROGRESSION &amp; ENEMIES</p><h3>Power with a worthy opponent</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Newly spawned enemies scale with stage, Endless depth, and character levels above eight. Health grows faster than damage; walking-speed growth is bounded and attack warnings are unchanged. Existing enemies are not healed or strengthened mid-fight when you level up.</li>
                <li>Enemy health scaling uses (1 + map index × 0.22) × (1 + Endless depth × 0.055 + (depth / 60)<sup>1.4</sup>) × (1 + levels above eight × 0.055)<sup>1.15</sup>. Campaign depth is zero; Endless depth counts waves beyond 30. Equipment, cards, forge purchases, and tree choices are not inputs to enemy scaling.</li>
                <li>Enemy damage grows more gradually with stage, depth, and level, instead of following the health multiplier. Boss base health grows by 380 per map index instead of 650 before the new scaling; elite damage amplification falls from 40% to 30%. Enemy counts remain capped at 40.</li>
                <li>Weapon forge ranks now add <strong>3 base damage and 2.5% damage</strong>, instead of 6 and 8%. Sunforged oath adds <strong>4%</strong> per rank instead of 5%; the percentage adds to the forge bonus rather than multiplying it.</li>
                <li>Equipment ratings are preserved, but their damage contribution now follows 60 × (√(1 + rating / 30) − 1), preventing early relic drops from overwhelming every other upgrade. The equipment panel shows effective damage. Level-up damage, health, XP pacing, gold rewards, forge prices, and the 32-point tree budget are unchanged.</li>
                <li>Reach caps: arrows 1,000 units; melee attacks 220; special and ward pulses 340; melee bursts 500. Area upgrades remain useful until their cap rather than eventually covering the entire arena.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">CLASS PARITY</p><h3>Three different strengths</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Ember Knight:</strong> attack interval 0.38s → 0.42s. Ember nova cooldown 5s → 6s, radius 190 → 175, damage 2.2× → 1.85×. Flame lance damage 2.4× → 2.1×. Still excels at close-range groups, but no longer clears them as cheaply.</li>
                <li><strong>Dawn Ranger:</strong> attack interval 0.48s → 0.34s. Sunburst volley cooldown 6s → 5.5s and arrow damage 0.9× → 1.05×. Piercing rain cooldown 9s → 8s, arrow damage 1.1× → 1.15×, and each arrow now penetrates a second target by default.</li>
                <li><strong>Iron Warden:</strong> innate armor 15% → 12%, attack interval 0.65s → 0.72s, reach 90 → 85. Iron quake cooldown 7s → 8s, radius 170 → 160, damage 2.8× → 2.2×. Fault line cooldown 10s → 11s, reach 280 → 250, damage 3.2× → 2.6×. Iron bastion pulse damage 1.2× → 1×. High base health remains its strength.</li>
                <li>Attack and special forge cooldown bonuses are 7% per rank instead of 12%. Melee special forge damage adds 0.12× per rank instead of 0.25×; Ranger retains 0.1×. Forge cleaves widen more slowly, preserving directional positioning.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">CLASS SKILL TREES</p><h3>Ember, briar, and iron</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Every class has its own node names and visual theme: circular ember paths for Knight, angular jade paths for Ranger, and heavy steel-blue nodes for Warden. Offensive effects use the class palette; Ranger arrows no longer inherit generic fire effects.</li>
                <li><strong>Knight:</strong> Backdraft replaces special-hit slowing with 16 units of knockback per rank, halved for bosses. Searing thrust adds 25% Flame lance damage against targets at or below half health. Cinder reprisal doubles the ward's opening damage instead of pushing enemies. Sunfire and regular-attack finishing remain.</li>
                <li><strong>Ranger:</strong> Trickshot makes Dawn shot ricochet once to a different living enemy within 180 units for 60% damage. Bodkin volley makes each special arrow penetrate one extra target. Deep penetration lets each Piercing rain arrow penetrate two extra targets instead of one.</li>
                <li>Piercing follow-through resolves along the arrow's remaining path at impact, retaining 70% damage per extra target. Ricochet and piercing tracers show the hit paths. One arrow cannot loop back into the same target. Briar snare combines ward knockback with a 15% walking slow for 1.5s; charges remain unaffected.</li>
                <li><strong>Warden:</strong> Shieldbreaker bypasses Sentinel resistance and adds 25% regular-attack damage against Sentinels. Seismic core adds 25% Iron quake damage inside half its radius. Crushing force adds 12% Fault line damage per rank against slowed targets, replacing extra critical chance. Bulldozer pushes enemies 100 units, or bosses 50.</li>
                <li>Existing node ranks and prerequisites are retained in the corresponding class branches. One point every two levels, 32 points maximum; all trees still cost eight points to complete. Defensive and recovery foundations remain shared where they serve the same purpose.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">HEALTH &amp; FLASKS</p><h3>Survival is part of the build</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Armor reduction caps at <strong>60%</strong> instead of 65%. Equipment contributes 60% of its armor rating, forge ranks add 3 armor instead of 5, and Unbroken vow adds 1.5 instead of 2.5 per rank. Existing health investments are preserved.</li>
                <li>Global cooldown recovery from cards, training, and Windwake caps at twice normal. Dodge recharge has a 0.9-second minimum at that cap, preventing permanent invulnerability. Class wards cannot refresh while active and leave at least two seconds without ward protection after expiry.</li>
                <li>Every five-wave checkpoint restores <strong>20% maximum health before difficulty healing penalties</strong> and grants <strong>two flask charges</strong>, up to five. Removed the duplicate full heal/refill on entering a stage. Campaign victory uses the same recovery rule.</li>
                <li>Random flask drops fall from 7% to 3%; bosses still guarantee a flask. Picking up a flask with full charges heals 8% maximum health before difficulty penalties instead of a full flask's worth. Flask healing upgrades, emergency-healing nodes, and regeneration remain valuable.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">LOOT &amp; ENDLESS FLOW</p><h3>Collect, save, keep fighting</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Attracted loot moves at 1,200 + 6 × distance units/second instead of 230. Attraction range and its upgrades remain intact. Loot no longer expires while waiting to be collected.</li>
                <li>All remaining drops are applied before every five-wave checkpoint save. The loot-count safety limit collects real rewards—including equipment and upgrade scrolls—instead of converting non-gold overflow into generic gold.</li>
                <li>Endless saves at every completed five-wave stage, then automatically begins the three-second travel countdown. No Continue button is required. Pause and the forge remain available during play.</li>
                <li>If a checkpoint save fails, progression stays safely stopped: use Save to retry. Loading an Endless checkpoint prepares travel but waits for Resume. Hidden or unfocused tabs stay paused after saving. Campaign travel still uses its normal confirmation.</li>
                <li>Existing saves, inventory, learned ranks, and class ranking records are retained. Updated formulas apply to existing heroes immediately; newly spawned enemies use the new scaling. Historical leaderboard builds remain snapshots of their original submissions, not recalculated Patch 004 records.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
    </article>

    <article id="patch-003" aria-labelledby="patch-003-title">
        <header class="patch-heading">
            <div class="patch-meta"><span class="badge">PATCH 003</span><a href="/patch-notes#patch-003" target="_self" aria-label="Link to patch 003">Permalink ↗</a></div>
            <h2 id="patch-003-title">Paths of Power</h2>
            <p>New skills and branching trees, more rewarding battles, independent forge prices, and a more readable Endless Watch.</p>
            <div class="tags"><span>Skills &amp; trees</span><span>New enemies</span><span>Endless fixes</span><span>Reward buffs</span><span>Forge</span><span>Critical hits</span></div>
        </header>
        <section class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">CLASS RANKINGS &amp; BUILDS</p><h3>A record for every class</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Each player now keeps a separate highest kill score for Ember Knight, Dawn Ranger, and Iron Warden within every difficulty and starting mode. Playing another class no longer replaces or hides that class's best. Ranks and ties are calculated independently per class.</li>
                <li>The rankings page shows all three class boards by default, with a class filter alongside difficulty and starting mode. Registered players appear on each board, with unplayed classes marked clearly.</li>
                <li>New records include expandable build details: character level, manual and automatic skill slots, learned tree nodes, forge upgrades, level-up cards, and post-forge training, including each upgrade's rank. These are snapshots when a higher score is submitted, not a view of the player's current character; lower or tied scores do not overwrite them.</li>
                <li>Existing records move to their recorded class without losing scores or timestamps. Pre-class records default to Ember Knight. Older builds and scores previously discarded for other classes cannot be reconstructed; missing build details are labeled rather than guessed.</li>
                <li>Score synchronization and retries now track each class independently. Build summaries are size- and rank-bounded; rankings remain browser-reported community records, not cheat-proof competition.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">CLEARER DIFFICULTIES</p><h3>Know where to start</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Hard:</strong> the easiest of the three tiers and recommended for a first run, despite its name. Healing restores 80% of its normal amount.</li>
                <li><strong>Nightmare:</strong> the middle tier, with more enemies that are tougher, faster, and hit harder than on Hard. Healing restores 60% of its normal amount.</li>
                <li><strong>Inferno:</strong> the hardest tier, with the most enemies and highest enemy health, damage, and speed. Healing restores only 40% of its normal amount.</li>
                <li>Run setup now explains the selected tier in plain language, and ranking filters use clearer labels. Difficulty names, balance multipliers, save compatibility, and existing score categories are unchanged.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">REWARD BUFFS</p><h3>A little more from every battle</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Enemy XP rewards increased by <strong>10%</strong> on Hard, Nightmare, and Inferno, in both Campaign and Endless. Applies to ordinary enemies, elites, and bosses; rewards round to whole points.</li>
                <li>After level 15, XP rewards gain another <strong>3% per level</strong>, capped at +60% at level 35. Next-level requirements grow by 22% instead of 35% after level 15; the 25,000 XP requirement cap remains. Existing XP is preserved.</li>
                <li>Gold rewards increased by <strong>10%</strong>, including gold pickups, equipment salvage, and overflow loot conversion. Fortune cards still multiply gold pickups; starting gold and existing balances are unchanged.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">POST-FORGE TRAINING</p><h3>Your stats, your prices</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Replaced the shared <strong>+250 gold</strong> increase with <strong>+100 gold per rank of the selected stat</strong>. Each stat's next price is 1,000 + 100 × its own rank. Buying damage no longer raises health, recovery, or any other stat's price.</li>
                <li>Existing ranks are retained and use the cheaper independent prices. Past purchases are not refunded.</li>
                <li>Added <strong>Widening ember</strong>: +1% attack reach and special radius/range per rank, including Ranger arrows. Reach is limited to the arena diagonal.</li>
                <li>Added <strong>Endless stride</strong>: +1% movement speed per rank, additive with movement-card bonuses and multiplied by Windwake. Dodge distance is unchanged.</li>
                <li>Added <strong>Unerring spark</strong>: +0.5 percentage points critical chance per rank, and <strong>Endless ruin</strong>: +2 percentage points critical damage per rank.</li>
                <li>The forge reminder now checks independently priced training options. These options join damage, health, and recovery after all regular forge upgrades are complete.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">CRITICAL HITS · CARDS · FORGE</p><h3>Make the next strike count</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>All classes now start with <strong>5% critical chance</strong> and <strong>150% critical damage</strong>. Each successful attack or special hit rolls independently, including each Ranger arrow hit. Critical damage numbers are highlighted and labeled CRIT.</li>
                <li>Added <strong>The surest spark</strong> card: +2 percentage points critical chance per rank, up to 10 ranks. Added <strong>A shattering dawn</strong>: +10 percentage points critical damage per rank, up to 10 ranks.</li>
                <li>Added regular forge upgrades <strong>Keen ember</strong> (+2 percentage points critical chance per rank; 60 gold starting price) and <strong>Ruin's edge</strong> (+10 percentage points critical damage per rank; 65 gold starting price). Both have eight ranks and use the existing regular-forge price curve.</li>
                <li>Cards, regular forge upgrades, and post-forge training stack. Total critical chance caps at <strong>75%</strong>; critical damage starts at 150%, so a +10-point upgrade makes a critical hit deal 160% of normal damage.</li>
                <li>Critical hits preserve Sentinel resistance for basic attacks and the existing resistance bypass for specials. Hero stats now show critical chance, critical damage, attack reach, and special radius/range.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">ACCESS &amp; TRANSITIONS</p><h3>Find your footing</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Fixed the forge and skill-unlock reminder strips blocking attacks and aiming at the bottom of the arena. Their text and backgrounds now pass clicks through to the canvas without selecting text; the reminder buttons remain interactive.</li>
                <li>A persistent Endless control now sits beside the arena controls. Unlocked profiles can start a veteran Endless run without hunting for an overlay button. At campaign victory it offers to continue the current character. Load a completed campaign save first to keep that character's equipment.</li>
                <li>Stage travel has a three-second countdown and fade. The next stage is prepared at the midpoint, while combat stays frozen until the countdown ends. New runs also receive three seconds to prepare.</li>
                <li>Resuming from the forge, skill panel, or pause gives a 2.5-second countdown. Enemies, attacks, hazards, buffs, and cooldowns do not advance during it. Nearby return-to-battle buttons make leaving the shops easier.</li>
                <li>A dismissible Patch 003 announcement links to these notes. Dismissal is remembered per user in this browser when local storage is available.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">COMBAT SKILLS &amp; CONTROLS</p><h3>Attack freely. Choose your skills.</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>J / left click:</strong> regular attack, always available without a skill slot. Its forge, card, critical, and tree upgrades still apply; it cannot be auto-cast.</li>
                <li><strong>Q:</strong> your selected manual combat skill, initially suggested as the class special. Only special, burst, and ward skills use slots: one manual and up to two automatic. The initial suggestion places burst in one auto slot and leaves the other empty. A skill cannot occupy two slots. Configure while paused through <strong>Skills &amp; loadout</strong>.</li>
                <li>Starting a campaign, starting a fresh Endless run, or continuing a completed campaign into Endless now requires an explicit <strong>starting loadout confirmation</strong>. Skipping tutorial tips goes to skill selection rather than starting combat. Cancel preserves the existing character.</li>
                <li>The third combat skill—the class ward—unlocks only at <strong>campaign stage 3 (wave 11)</strong>, or <strong>Echo 11 (wave 41)</strong> in a fresh Endless run. A completed campaign character keeps the unlock when entering Endless. Before that point, the ward cannot be slotted, cast, trained, purchased at the forge, or offered as a new level-up card.</li>
                <li>Unlocking the ward shows an on-canvas <strong>Slot skill</strong> reminder after the travel countdown. It opens the loadout panel and pauses combat. The reminder stays pending until the ward is slotted or Later is selected; acknowledgement is retained by the next save. It does not resize the canvas or overlap the forge reminder.</li>
                <li><strong>Space:</strong> dodge. <strong>E:</strong> drink a flask. Both remain upgradeable, always use their own controls, and can never be automatically cast. Touch controls retain separate attack, dodge, flask, and manual-skill buttons.</li>
                <li>Auto casts have <strong>60% longer cooldowns</strong>. Offense aims at the nearest enemy in range; auto wards wait for nearby danger and avoid overwriting active protection. Updated the HUD, tutorial, loadout selectors, control guide, and low-health prompt to match.</li>
                <li><strong>Ember Knight:</strong> Flame lance deals 2.4× weapon damage along a narrow 380-range beam (8s base cooldown). Ember aegis deals 0.8× in 125 radius (12s cooldown).</li>
                <li><strong>Dawn Ranger:</strong> Piercing rain fires seven shield-piercing arrows, each dealing 1.1× weapon damage at 760 range (9s cooldown; each arrow hits once). Briar ward deals 0.65× in 180 radius (11s cooldown).</li>
                <li><strong>Iron Warden:</strong> Fault line deals 3.2× weapon damage in a forward 280-range cone (10s cooldown). Iron bastion deals 1.2× in 150 radius (14s cooldown).</li>
                <li>All wards grant <strong>40% damage reduction for three seconds</strong> before duration bonuses, stacking multiplicatively with armor and other protection. Class-colored shields, beams, cones, and arrow fans show the new skills in action.</li>
                <li>New eight-rank forge entries: <strong>Breaking dawn</strong> adds +10% burst damage, +8 range, and +8% recovery per rank; <strong>Last sanctuary</strong> adds +8% ward damage, +8 radius, +0.2s protection, and +8% recovery per rank.</li>
                <li>New ten-rank cards: <strong>Dawn unleashed</strong> adds +6% burst damage per rank; <strong>A shelter in embers</strong> adds +0.15s ward protection per rank. Existing damage, cooldown, and area training also supports the new damaging skills.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">LIMITED SKILL POINTS</p><h3>Choose a path for every skill</h3>
            <p>Earn one point <strong>every two levels</strong>, starting at level 2, capped at <strong>32 total</strong> instead of 12. Each complete tree costs eight points: fully upgrade three trees at level 48 or four at level 64. Existing characters automatically receive their expanded level-appropriate budget without losing learned ranks. Each rank costs one point; choices remain with this character.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Each action now has its own upgrade path instead of sharing Empower, Expand, Ignite, and Flow. Fire damage is exclusive to the class-special tree; wards, dodges, and flasks no longer produce generic fire pulses.</li>
                <li><strong>Regular attack:</strong> Honed edge adds +8% damage per rank; Long reach adds +10% reach or arrow range per rank; Finisher deals 25% more damage against enemies at or below 35% health; Weapon tempo adds +6% regular-attack speed per rank. Silver trails and crimson finishing marks distinguish weapon upgrades.</li>
                <li><strong>Class special:</strong> Amplify adds +10% damage per rank; Resonance adds +10% radius or volley range per rank; Sunfire adds 15% fire damage; Chilling wake slows enemy walking by 15% per rank for 1.5 seconds without slowing charges. Golden pulses, fiery arrows, and frost rings show the learned effects.</li>
                <li><strong>Burst:</strong> Concentrate adds +12% damage per rank. The shape branch widens Flame lance by 20% per rank, adds two Piercing rain arrows per rank, or widens each side of Fault line by 10 degrees per rank. Giant slayer adds 25% damage against elites and bosses; Overdrive adds 10 percentage points critical chance per rank to burst hits only, respecting the 75% cap.</li>
                <li><strong>Ward:</strong> Bulwark adds five percentage points damage reduction per rank, up to 55%; Enduring ward adds 0.6 seconds protection per rank; Repulsion pushes enemies back 60 units, or 30 for bosses; Safe haven restores 2% maximum health per rank when protection expires. Shields, repulsion waves, and healing crosses replace offensive fire upgrades.</li>
                <li><strong>Dodge:</strong> Ghoststep adds 0.04 seconds invulnerability per rank; Long stride adds 0.025 seconds roll duration per rank; Windstep grants +20% walking speed for 1.2 seconds from the dodge start without changing roll speed; Quick footing adds +10% dodge cooldown recovery per rank. Evasion outlines and wind trails identify these mobility upgrades.</li>
                <li><strong>Life flask:</strong> Distill adds +10% healing per rank; Triage adds +12% healing per rank when drinking at or below 35% health; Iron tonic grants 25% damage reduction for two seconds, stacking multiplicatively with other protection; Renewal regenerates 4% maximum health per rank over two seconds and refreshes rather than stacks. Healing respects difficulty penalties; emergency crosses, tonic shields, and regeneration auras show the effects.</li>
                <li>The skill-tree interface uses circular nodes joined by visible branching paths. Each root has three ranks, its two branches have two and one ranks, and the final node has two ranks. Branches require the root; the final node requires either branch. Learned and available paths have distinct highlights. Hover or keyboard focus reveals upgrade descriptions, rank costs, and unmet prerequisites. On touch, tap once to inspect and again to purchase; locked nodes remain inspectable.</li>
                <li>Individual node rank limits are unchanged; the larger point budget supports deeper builds while still requiring choices across the six trees. Reduced-motion preferences preserve readable warnings while suppressing cosmetic movement.</li>
                <li>Fixed skill-node tooltips remaining visible after allocating a point. Successful allocation dismisses the tooltip without removing keyboard focus; hovering again, refocusing, or tapping to inspect reopens it.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">ENEMY VARIETY</p><h3>Read the next strike</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Duelists, from wave 4:</strong> cyan twin-blade fighters flank before a marked 0.65-second forward slash.</li>
                <li><strong>Artillerists, from wave 13:</strong> violet mortar units mark a ground circle for 1.1 seconds before a 90-radius blast. The aim stays fixed: leave the circle before impact.</li>
                <li>Ordinary melee attacks now wind up with visible arcs and locked aim: 0.55 seconds normally and 0.8 seconds for brutes. Ranged enemies show casting warnings; aimed projectile attacks lock direction during wind-up.</li>
                <li>Removed the ranged projectile aiming lines. Casting animations and bright projectiles remain; melee arcs, charge lanes, and mortar-area warnings are unchanged.</li>
                <li>Enemy projectiles have bright pink-white cores, contrasting dark outlines, glow, and longer directional trails. They render above combat effects and the vignette. Damage, speed, and collision hitboxes are unchanged.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">SAVE COMPATIBILITY</p><h3>Keep your existing character</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Save format 12 stores the action-specific trees, chilling, Windstep, tonic protection, and regeneration alongside existing progression, loadouts, and ward-unlock acknowledgement. Formats 1–11 remain supported without rerolling pending cards.</li>
                <li>Previously spent generic-tree points are returned for reassignment to the new trees. Equipment, forge upgrades, and cards are preserved; new timed effects start inactive. Skill-point budgets use the expanded one-per-two-levels rate, capped at 32.</li>
                <li>Older characters below the ward's unlock stage keep their purchased forge upgrades, but the ward is removed from their loadout and active protection ends until the stage requirement is met. Existing unlocked, equipped wards stay equipped without a redundant reminder.</li>
                <li>Older version 9 loadouts lose attack/dodge/flask auto assignments, retain valid combat-skill assignments, and default Q to the class special if the old manual slot held a regular action. Empty auto slots remain empty after migration; existing upgrades are preserved.</li>
                <li>Characters that had completed their old regular forge receive newly added regular entries fully ranked, preserving post-forge access. Other older characters start those new upgrades at zero. Existing scroll indices are preserved.</li>
                <li>Browser and server validation enforce point budgets, prerequisites, unique slots, ranks, and travel state. Skill and loadout changes autosave at checkpoints; elsewhere use Save or reach the next checkpoint.</li>
                <li>Existing upgrade-scroll indices are preserved, and new scrolls can award critical upgrades. Both browser and server validation support the expanded progression.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
    </article>

    <article id="patch-002" aria-labelledby="patch-002-title">
        <header class="patch-heading">
            <div class="patch-meta"><span class="badge">PATCH 002</span><a href="/patch-notes#patch-002" target="_self" aria-label="Link to patch 002">Permalink ↗</a></div>
            <h2 id="patch-002-title">A Steadier Arena</h2>
            <p>A focused fix for canvas resizing and page movement during cards, notifications, and menu transitions.</p>
            <div class="tags"><span>Bug fixes</span><span>Arena view</span><span>UI stability</span></div>
        </header>
        <section class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">BUG FIXES</p><h3>Keep the battlefield in place</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Pinned an Open forge reminder to the bottom of the canvas during combat whenever at least one unfinished upgrade is affordable (or stat training after completing the forge). It overlays the arena without resizing it, stays out of card and setup screens, and pauses combat when opened.</li>
                <li>Repaired malformed forge-heading markup that displayed stray text and misplaced the reminder inside the heading.</li>
                <li>Stabilized objective, power-up, and Pause / Resume rows so changing text does not shift the arena. Long power-up lists scroll horizontally; objective and power-up text also have full-text tooltips.</li>
                <li>Gave the arena its own fixed aspect ratio, independent of canvas rendering resolution. Overlays keep their scrolling and animation inside the arena, with reserved scrollbar space to prevent card-width jumps.</li>
                <li>Selecting a level-up card no longer triggers automatic arena recentering. Disabled browser scroll anchoring within the game to prevent dynamic panels from pulling the page.</li>
                <li>Kept window resizing, Focus view toggling, explicit Recenter, and start/resume alignment available. Gameplay, balance, and saves are unchanged.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
    </article>

    <article id="patch-001" aria-labelledby="patch-title">
        <header class="patch-heading">
            <div class="patch-meta"><span class="badge">PATCH 001</span><a href="/patch-notes#patch-001" target="_self" aria-label="Link to patch 001">Permalink ↗</a></div>
            <h2 id="patch-title">The Relentless Watch</h2>
            <p>A harder journey, a place in the rankings, and more reasons to visit the forge. This entry covers the ranking-system release and all subsequent changes in this session.</p>
            <div class="tags"><span>Rankings</span><span>Difficulty</span><span>Balance</span><span>New enemy</span><span>Quality of life</span><span>Graphics overhaul</span></div>
        </header>
        <nav class="contents" aria-label="Patch sections">
            <a href="/patch-notes#classes" target="_self">Character classes</a>
            <a href="/patch-notes#rankings" target="_self">Rankings</a><a href="/patch-notes#difficulty" target="_self">Difficulty</a><a href="/patch-notes#survival" target="_self">Survival</a><a href="/patch-notes#cards" target="_self">Cards</a><a href="/patch-notes#progression" target="_self">Progression &amp; forge</a><a href="/patch-notes#combat" target="_self">Combat &amp; tutorial</a><a href="/patch-notes#arena-view" target="_self">Arena view &amp; controls</a><a href="/patch-notes#fixes" target="_self">Fixes &amp; saves</a>
            <a href="/patch-notes#graphics" target="_self">Graphics finale</a>
        </nav>

        <section id="rankings" class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">NEW FEATURE</p><h3>Hall of Embers: player rankings</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Added a dedicated <a href="/rankings">rankings page</a>. Signed-in Windows users are registered automatically and appear with zero points before submitting a score.</li>
                <li>Score is enemies slain. Each player's highest score is kept on the server for each difficulty and starting mode; a lower score never replaces a personal best.</li>
                <li>Separate boards for <strong>Campaign journey</strong>, <strong>Veteran Endless start</strong>, and <strong>Endless after campaign</strong>.</li>
                <li>Entering Endless after a campaign preserves the campaign record and starts a new score counter. Campaign-equipped characters do not compete on the veteran-start board.</li>
                <li>Added difficulty/mode filters, shared ranks for tied scores, record timestamps in UTC, a highlighted current-player row, and manual refresh.</li>
                <li>Scores sync every 15 seconds and on pauses, checkpoints, death, and run replacement. Failed submissions retry while the page remains open.</li>
                <li>The game links to rankings in a new tab so checking the table does not discard a run.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="callout">Community rankings use browser-reported scores. Player identity is authenticated, but the game is not a cheat-proof competitive system. Saves from before the difficulty system remain playable and unranked.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="classes" class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">TWO NEW CLASSES · CLASS STATS · RANKINGS</p><h3>Three ways to carry the dawn</h3>
            <p>Choose a character class alongside difficulty when starting a new campaign or veteran Endless run. Each class has its own base stats, weapon, primary attack, and special. Continuing a campaign character into Endless keeps its class and equipment.</p>
            <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Starting class statistics" tabindex="0">
                <table>
                    <caption>Fresh-character stats before equipment, boons, or forge upgrades. Damage is base weapon damage; cooldowns are before recovery bonuses.</caption>
                    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Class</th><th scope="col">Health</th><th scope="col">Damage</th><th scope="col">Speed</th><th scope="col">Innate armor</th><th scope="col">Attack interval</th><th scope="col">Special cooldown</th><th scope="col">Dodge cooldown</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Ember Knight</th><td>100</td><td>18</td><td>220</td><td>0%</td><td>0.38s</td><td>5s</td><td>1.8s</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Dawn Ranger — new</th><td>80</td><td>15</td><td>245</td><td>0%</td><td>0.48s</td><td>6s</td><td>1.6s</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Iron Warden — new</th><td>150</td><td>24</td><td>180</td><td>15%</td><td>0.65s</td><td>7s</td><td>2.4s</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Ember Knight:</strong> the existing balanced melee class. Cleave has 100 base reach; Ember nova hits nearby enemies within 190 base radius for 2.2× weapon damage.</li>
                <li><strong>Dawn Ranger:</strong> a mobile ranged skirmisher with lower health and damage. Dawn shot fires an aimed arrow up to 680 arena units. Sunburst volley fires five arrows in a fan, each dealing 0.9× weapon damage before upgrades. Each arrow hits one enemy; volley arrows bypass Sentinel resistance, while basic arrows do not.</li>
                <li><strong>Iron Warden:</strong> an armored bruiser with slower movement and attack recovery. Hammer sweep has 90 base reach. Iron quake hits nearby enemies within 170 base radius for 2.8× weapon damage. Innate armor shares the existing 65% damage-reduction cap.</li>
                <li>Added distinct bow, hammer/shield, outfit colors, and friendly-arrow visuals. Arrow collision checks cover the full travel segment to avoid skipping enemies at speed.</li>
                <li>The class selector shows a stat preview. The hero panel shows the current class, role, movement speed, innate armor, and base attack/dodge timings. Skill names, tutorial guidance, equipment names, and forge hints adapt to the class.</li>
                <li>Shared Cleave/Nova forge upgrades and cards improve the equivalent class attack and special. Ranger reach/radius bonuses extend arrow range; its volley damage grows by 0.1× weapon damage per special-forge rank, rather than the melee specials' 0.25×.</li>
                <li>Veteran Endless kits preserve class differences: each class gains 201 health and 44 base damage over its fresh-character values, plus the existing veteran equipment and forge kit. Existing Ember Knight veteran stats are unchanged.</li>
                <li>Added <strong>Class used</strong> to rankings. The class is stored with the highest score for each player, difficulty, and starting mode. A lower or tied score from another class does not relabel the earlier record.</li>
                <li>Save format 7 preserves class identity and arrows in flight. Earlier saves and pre-class records default to Ember Knight without resetting accumulated progress. Class cannot be switched on a loaded character.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="difficulty" class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">NEW FEATURE · ENEMY BUFFS</p><h3>Choose your challenge</h3>
            <p>Added Hard, Nightmare, and Inferno. Difficulty is selected when starting a new campaign or entering Endless, then stays locked throughout the run. Campaign stage transitions never ask you to choose again.</p>
            <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Difficulty multipliers" tabindex="0">
                <table>
                    <caption>Multipliers applied to the enemy baseline; healing penalties apply to flasks, health pickups, and Ashdrinker.</caption>
                    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Difficulty</th><th scope="col">Enemy health</th><th scope="col">Damage</th><th scope="col">Speed</th><th scope="col">Healing retained</th><th scope="col">Extra enemies</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Hard</th><td>×1.30</td><td>×1.20</td><td>×1.08</td><td>80%</td><td>+2</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Nightmare</th><td>×1.70</td><td>×1.50</td><td>×1.16</td><td>60%</td><td>+4</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Inferno</th><td>×2.20</td><td>×1.90</td><td>×1.24</td><td>40%</td><td>+6</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li>Maximum initial wave population increased from <strong>36 to 40</strong>, including difficulty additions.</li>
                <li>Elites begin appearing at <strong>wave 6 instead of wave 12</strong>.</li>
                <li>Added chapter-based enemy scaling and strengthened Endless scaling as the watch continues.</li>
                <li>A dedicated setup panel now explains difficulty and confirms a new run. Cancel keeps the current character.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="survival" class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">SURVIVAL NERFS · FLASK BUFFS</p><h3>Healing must be earned</h3>
            <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Survival balance changes" tabindex="0">
                <table>
                    <caption>Before this session → final values</caption>
                    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Stat</th><th scope="col">Before</th><th scope="col">Now</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Invulnerability after taking damage</th><td>0.4 seconds</td><td>0.3 seconds</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Automatic level-up healing</th><td>15 health</td><td>None</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Automatic level-up flask refill</th><td>+1 charge</td><td>None</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Health-drop chance on eligible loot rolls</th><td>23%</td><td>8%</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Health pickup base healing</th><td>22</td><td>12, then difficulty penalty</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Flask carrying capacity</th><td>3</td><td>5</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li>Ordinary kills no longer trigger Ashdrinker healing. Only elite and boss kills qualify.</li>
                <li>Added <strong>Life flask pickups</strong>: a 7% drop chance on kills, with a guaranteed flask from bosses.</li>
                <li>Picking up a flask below capacity adds one charge. At five charges, the pickup immediately heals for the same amount as drinking one flask, without consuming a charge.</li>
                <li>Flask healing uses the existing formula: the greater of 30 health or 22% of maximum health plus 6 percentage points per flask-forge rank, followed by the difficulty multiplier.</li>
                <li>Checkpoints still fully restore health. Checkpoints and flask-forge purchases now refill to five charges; fresh characters still begin with three.</li>
                <li>Updated flask pickup labels, charge displays, card descriptions, and control hints.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="cards" class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">CARD NERFS</p><h3>Oaths remain powerful—not overwhelming</h3>
            <p>Bonuses below are per rank. Existing rank limits are unchanged. Ashdrinker values are shown before difficulty penalties.</p>
            <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Card balance changes" tabindex="0">
                <table>
                    <caption>Before this session → final values</caption>
                    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Card</th><th scope="col">Before</th><th scope="col">Now</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Sunforged oath</th><td>+6% weapon damage</td><td>+5%</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Heart of the dawn</th><td>+20 maximum health; heal 20</td><td>+16 maximum health; heal 16</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Unbroken vow</th><td>+3 armor</td><td>+2.5 armor; 65% reduction cap unchanged</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Wayfarer's instinct</th><td>+5% movement speed</td><td>+4%</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Quiet flame</th><td>+6% cooldown recovery</td><td>+5%</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Widening sunrise</th><td>+12 nova radius</td><td>+10</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Horizon cutter</th><td>+8 cleave reach</td><td>+6</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Call of the fallen</th><td>+20 loot attraction range</td><td>+16</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">Ashdrinker</th><td>0.35 health per kill</td><td>0.8 per elite/boss kill only, then difficulty penalty</td></tr>
                        <tr><th scope="row">The ferryman's due</th><td>+10% pickup gold</td><td>+8%; salvage unchanged</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="progression" class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">PROGRESSION · FORGE</p><h3>Gold has a purpose beyond the last upgrade</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>First level-up requirement increased from <strong>40 to 60 XP</strong>. Subsequent requirements grow by <strong>35% instead of 30%</strong>, with the per-level XP requirement ceiling raised from <strong>5,000 to 25,000</strong>. This is not a character-level cap.</li>
                <li>Normal enemy XP reduced from <strong>14 + 6 per chapter index</strong> to <strong>10 + 4 per chapter index</strong>. Boss XP reduced from <strong>100 + 50 per chapter index</strong> to <strong>70 + 35 per chapter index</strong>. Chapter indices start at zero; elite XP remains doubled.</li>
                <li>Veteran Endless characters start with a next-level requirement of <strong>1,600 XP instead of 700</strong>.</li>
                <li>Removed guaranteed periodic upgrade scrolls. Scrolls now have a <strong>30% boss chance</strong> or <strong>4% elite chance</strong>; ordinary enemies no longer drop them. Earlier in this session, the periodic interval had first been reduced from every 13 kills to every 25.</li>
                <li>Added an actionable forge banner when an upgrade is affordable and a highlighted Forge button. Opening the forge pauses combat.</li>
                <li>Completing <strong>every regular forge item</strong> unlocks <strong>Beyond the forge</strong>, an uncapped stat-training shop.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="stat-grid">
                <section><h4>Ember might</h4><p>+2 base weapon damage per purchase.</p></section>
                <section><h4>Enduring heart</h4><p>+5 maximum health and restore 5 health per purchase.</p></section>
                <section><h4>Battle rhythm</h4><p>+0.5% skill cooldown recovery per purchase.</p></section>
            </div>
            <p class="callout">Training starts at <strong>1,000 gold</strong>. Every purchase adds <strong>250 gold</strong> to the next price across all three stats. Training is saved with the character; checkpoint purchases autosave, while purchases elsewhere require a manual save or the next checkpoint.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="combat" class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">NEW ENEMY · ANIMATIONS · TUTORIAL</p><h3>Watch the lane. Dodge the charge.</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Added <strong>Lancers from wave 8</strong>. They mark a long amber lane, wait <strong>1.1 seconds</strong>, then dash toward the far arena edge at <strong>1,200 arena units per second</strong>.</li>
                <li>The warning direction stays fixed during wind-up. Step sideways before the charge; Lancer dash hits deal <strong>1.5× their normal damage</strong>.</li>
                <li>Added player footstep cycles, sword-swing motion, and a nova casting pulse.</li>
                <li>Added mob walking cycles, attack/casting lunges, wind-up poses, and dash trails for the player and charging enemies.</li>
                <li>Reduced-motion preferences suppress the new cosmetic bobbing, swings, and trails while keeping danger warnings visible.</li>
                <li>Added a skippable three-part walkthrough for fresh campaign and veteran Endless runs: movement/combat, dodging/flasks, and forge/progression. Gameplay waits until setup is complete.</li>
                <li>Updated journal threats, control hints, and forge explanations to introduce the new mechanics.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="arena-view" class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">ARENA VIEW · CONTROL POLISH</p><h3>Keep your eyes on the fight</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Added <strong>Focus view</strong>, enabled by default and remembered on this browser. It places character information below the arena in separate, readable cards: four columns on wide screens, two on tablets, and one on phones, with larger text and compact hero stats. It sizes the canvas to the available window height, including room for combat controls. Turn it off for the wider page layout.</li>
                <li>Added an arena toolbar with <strong>Focus view</strong>, <strong>Recenter</strong>, and <strong>Pause / Resume</strong>, so these controls remain near the game rather than only in the page header.</li>
                <li>Starting, resuming, and opening setup now reveal the arena with only the scroll adjustment needed. A fully visible arena is left in place; no continuous scroll snapping fights manual browsing.</li>
                <li>Mouse-wheel and trackpad scrolling over the combat area is blocked while the canvas has keyboard focus and combat is active. Page Up, Page Down, Home, and End cannot accidentally move the page in that state. Pause to scroll normally; browser zoom shortcuts and scrolling elsewhere remain available.</li>
                <li>Card and tutorial overlays contain their own scrolling, preventing a scroll at the end of a menu from moving the page underneath.</li>
                <li>Focus view adapts to window resizing and mobile viewport changes. Canvas proportions are preserved on narrow screens instead of being stretched by a forced minimum height. Very short windows can still scroll to reach all controls.</li>
                <li>Opening the forge scrolls only as far as necessary, and respects reduced-motion preferences. Resuming returns the view to combat.</li>
                <li>Added a steady <strong>low-health warning at 25% health or below</strong>, with a subtle arena border and a hint based on whether any flasks remain. No flashing effect.</li>
                <li>Skill buttons now distinguish recovering skills visually. Buttons are disabled outside combat; the flask button also explains when charges are empty or health is already full. Holding attack still works through cooldown recovery. Disabled buttons use a not-allowed cursor rather than a waiting spinner, and disabled game buttons no longer show enabled-style hover effects.</li>
                <li>Corrected the initial XP display to show the current 60-XP first-level requirement.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="fixes" class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">BUG FIXES · QUALITY OF LIFE</p><h3>A smoother watch</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Removed difficulty selection from stage checkpoints. New-run setup and Endless entry are the only places to change it.</li>
                <li>Added keyboard focus handling to the setup walkthrough, including Tab navigation, Escape to cancel, and returning focus to the arena after starting.</li>
                <li>Kept the card overlay visible while its exit animation completes instead of hiding it on the next HUD update.</li>
                <li>Prevented checkpoint autosaves from being marked handled while the setup panel blocks saving.</li>
                <li>Lancer collision checks cover the full movement segment so fast dashes do not skip through the player without registering a hit.</li>
                <li>Reset player movement velocity on chapter relocation to prevent drift after arriving.</li>
                <li>Added save-format versions 5 and 6 for difficulty, ranking mode, mastery ranks, Endless score baselines, Lancers, and flask pickups. Older save versions remain supported; pre-difficulty saves stay unranked.</li>
                <li>Added matching API validation for the new save fields, five-flask capacity, and dash data.</li>
                <li>Added this dedicated patch-notes page, with section links, stat comparison tables, and navigation from the game and rankings.</li>
                <li>Fixed patch-note section links and “Back to patch summary” resolving to the home page. They now stay on the patch-notes route in the same tab.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="callout">Existing accumulated character stats are not retroactively rebuilt. For a clean comparison of the new leveling and growth rules, start a fresh run. The current card formulas apply to existing boon ranks.</p>
        </section>
        <section id="graphics" class="patch-section">
            <p class="eyebrow">FINAL ENTRY · GRAPHICS OVERHAUL</p><h3>BEAUTIFY THE GRAPHICS!</h3>
            <p>The closing update to Patch 001 gives the watch a richer, illustrated 2D look without changing its combat rules.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Richer arenas:</strong> beveled stone tiles, chapter-tinted lighting, etched border details, and additional scenery—branch-like patterns in the Hollow, water pools in the marsh, furnace veins in the Citadel, faceted ice in the Expanse, and arcane markings in the Archive and Rift.</li>
                <li><strong>Detailed heroes:</strong> shaded armor, layered cloaks, boots, shoulder plates, and class insignia. The Knight gains a crested helmet and highlighted blade; the Ranger gains a hood, quiver, and recurved bow; the Warden gains heavy plate, an ornate shield, and a forged hammer.</li>
                <li><strong>More dimensional enemies:</strong> shaded bodies and heads, folded caster robes, brighter material edges on charging enemies, and clearer creature silhouettes.</li>
                <li><strong>Distinct loot artwork:</strong> coins, glass flasks with red liquid, health crosses, shields, class-appropriate weapons, parchment upgrade scrolls, and faceted blessing gems replace generic pickup squares. Important pickups have subtle hover motion and outlined labels.</li>
                <li><strong>Combat effects:</strong> filled slash trails with bright edges, layered expanding spell rings, and outlined damage numbers. Enemy bolts now have warm-colored trails to distinguish them from the Ranger's green/gold arrows.</li>
                <li><strong>Atmosphere and framing:</strong> drifting chapter-colored motes, a less aggressive edge vignette, an ornamented boss-health frame, and a lightly gilded arena with richer toolbar and vitality-panel backgrounds.</li>
                <li><strong>Sharper rendering:</strong> the active canvas adapts to display density, capped at twice the logical resolution. Floor artwork is generated at up to 1.5× resolution; aiming and movement still use the same 1100 × 650 game coordinates.</li>
                <li><strong>Bounded visual resources:</strong> chapter backgrounds are cached, small shading/body sprites use a bounded cache, and ambient motes are limited to 28. All artwork is generated locally; no external art downloads or additional libraries.</li>
                <li><strong>Readability first:</strong> environmental particles stay beneath hazard telegraphs. Reduced-motion preferences stop cosmetic drifting, bobbing, and weapon movement while retaining meaningful combat feedback.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="callout">Visual changes only: no changes to class balance, hitboxes, damage, progression, rankings, or save format.</p>
        </section>
    </article>
